1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
I'm Mario Ritter with the V.O.A. Special English Agriculture Report.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Cacao or cocoa trees grow in hot rainy areas of Africa, Asia and Central and South America.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Their beans are used to make cocoa powder, cocoa butter, and of course chocolate.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
There are five to six million growers, maybe more.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Many are poor family farmers with only a few hectares.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
West Africa produces more than half of all cocoa beans.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Ivory Coast leads the world in production, followed by its neighbor Ghana.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The trees are usually in their fifth year when they start to grow the pods that contain the beans.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The trees produce the most pods when they are 10, but they are still productive long after that.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Workers use large knives to cut the lower pods and long tools to remove pods from high on the trees.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Later they break open the pods to remove the beans.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:27,000
A half gram of chocolate requires about 400 beans.

13
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:34,000
The World Coco Foundation says an average pod contains 20 to 50 beans.

14
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:43,000
And experts say growers may lose perhaps one third of their harvest to disease and insects.

15
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
But now scientists have genetic maps of two kinds of cocoa trees.

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:01,000
These genomes are mostly complete and could lead scientists to new ways to increase production and prevent disease.

17
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Mapping genes is the first step to understanding an organism.

18
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Next comes learning the job of each gene.

19
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:22,000
The American Food Company Mars took the lead in paying for mapping the genes of the Foresterro Coco Tree.

20
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The Foresterro provides 80 to 90 percent of the world's cocoa beans.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Mars depends on those beans for its M&Ms and other chocolate candies.

22
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:47,000
The company's research partners included several universities and the United States Department of Agriculture.

23
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:56,000
The average West African cocoa farmer produces about 400 kilos of beans per hectare.

24
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:06,000
But Howard-Yana Shapiro, head of plant science and external research at Mars, thinks that science could greatly increase the yield.

25
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:18,000
A competitor of Mars, Hershey's, supported the gene mapping of the Criolo, a far less common cocoa tree.

26
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Sirad, a French government research center, led scientists from six countries in creating that genome.

27
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:43,000
We'll talk more about the cocoa industry next week when we look at efforts to help child laborers in Ivory Coast and Ghana.

28
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
For VOA Special English, I'm Mario Ritter.

29
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:57,000
You can read and listen to our reports at VOASpecialEnglish.com.

